    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, the compensation idea is interesting. but it's just such a weird, persistent feeling. it doesn't feel like a \"limitation\" or a \"weakness\" so much as it feels like this external, mischievous presence is just constantly tripping them up. like, it's not a big, obvious thing like a hip issue, but more like dozens of tiny, annoying little disruptions that jumble everything up at the most critical moments. it’s almost like they’re being pranked by their own body. do you think there's any scientific way to describe that kind of constant, small-scale \"mischief\" or disruption in the motor pathways?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When it feels like a constant, tiny, mischievous disruption, you might be touching upon concepts related to proprioceptive noise or subtle afferent feedback issues. Proprioception is your body's internal GPS, and if the signals coming from the joints and muscles are slightly jumbled—like a poorly tuned radio signal—the brain gets confused about where the body actually is. This \"noise\" can manifest as that feeling of being constantly tripped up, even if the actual structural issue is minuscule.\n\nIt's less a \"prank\" and more like the feedback loop is getting crossed. Try introducing highly specific, low-load drills that force extreme focus on *one* tiny segment of the movement, like controlling the eccentric phase of the descent with exaggerated slowness. This forces the nervous system to override the background noise by demanding hyper-accurate input.",
      "source": "target",
